  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is a fixed pivot?",
      "a": "A fixed pivot is a pin that holds a bar to a base, so the bar can turn about it but the pin itself does not move. A moving pivot joins two bars and moves with them.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the difference between a fixed and a moving pivot?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does a link bar do?",
      "a": "A link bar is a rigid strip that carries a push or a pull from one pivot to another. Its length and pivot positions decide how the motion changes.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a linkage made of?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a reverse motion linkage?",
      "a": "It is a bar with a fixed pivot in the middle. When one end is pushed, the other end moves the opposite way, so input and output move in reverse.",
      "asks": [
        "How can pushing make something pull?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a bell crank?",
      "a": "A bell crank is an L-shaped bar with a fixed pivot at the corner. It turns a push in one direction into a push at 90 degrees, as in bicycle brakes and old door bells.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you turn motion round a corner with a linkage?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where are linkages used?",
      "a": "Linkages are found in folding chairs, windscreen wipers, lamps, scissor lifts, pop-up books, bicycle brakes and robot arms.",
      "asks": [
        "What uses linkages?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "In a reverse motion linkage with the pivot in the centre, input and output move the same distance in opposite directions.",
    "Moving the fixed pivot closer to the input makes the output move further than the input."
  ],
